WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.260 --> 00:00:05.260
In 1989, Japan’s Shinkansen Bullet Train
had a problem.

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:10.059
It was fast — really fast — like, pushing
170 miles per hour fast.

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:13.110
But every time it exited a tunnel — it was
loud.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.720
The noise was coming from
a variety of sources, but whenever a train

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:22.590
sped into a tunnel, it pushed waves of atmospheric
pressure through the other end.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:27.630
The air exited tunnels with a sonic boom that
could be heard 400 meters away.

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:31.040
In dense residential areas, that was a huge
problem.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:35.820
So, an engineering team was brought in to
design a quieter, faster, and more efficient

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:36.540
train.

00:00:36.680 --> 00:00:41.579
And they had one secret weapon: Eiji Nakatsu — the
general manager of the technical development

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.900
department — was a birdwatcher.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:51.899
Different components of the redesigned bullet
train were based on different birds.

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:56.120
Owls inspired the pantograph — that’s
the rig that connects the train to the electric

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.780
wires above.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:03.180
Nakatsu modeled the redesign after their feathers,
reducing noise by using the same serrations

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:08.140
and curvature that allow them to silently
swoop down to catch prey.

00:01:08.140 --> 00:01:13.640
The Adelie Penguin — whose smooth body allows
it to swim and slide effortlessly — inspired

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.960
the pantograph’s supporting shaft, redesigned
for lower wind resistance.

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:21.720
And perhaps most notable of all was the Kingfisher.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:28.110
The Kingfisher is a bird that dives into water
to catch its prey.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:32.420
The unique shape of its beak allows it to
do that while barely making a splash.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:34.930
Nakatsu took that shape to the design table.

00:01:34.930 --> 00:01:39.479
The team shot bullets shaped like different
train nose models down a pipe to measure pressure

00:01:39.479 --> 00:01:42.890
waves, and dropped them in water to measure
the splash size.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:48.140
The quietest nose design was the one modeled
most closely after the Kingfisher’s beak.

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.960
When the redesign debuted in 1997, it was 10% faster, used 15% less electricity,

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:57.560
and stayed under the 70 dB noise limit in residential areas.

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.220
And it did all that with the wings of an owl,
the belly of a penguin, and the nose of a

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:03.420
Kingfisher.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.060
There’s a name for design like this.

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:09.780
It’s called biomimicry.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:19.940
The people who design our world usually never
take a biology class, believe it or not.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.900
So they're novices in how the world works.

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:27.900
That’s Janine Benyus.

00:02:27.910 --> 00:02:32.430
Back in 1997, she wrote the book that coined
the term “Biomimicry”.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:37.970
It told the story of the innovations in computing,
energy, and health that were inspired by structures

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:38.920
in the natural world.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:40.920
Stick like a gecko.

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.920
Compute like a cell.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.220
Even  run a business like a redwood forest.

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:51.200
Benyus has since worked as a consultant for
various companies, trying to get them to understand

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.120
how to take design ideas from nature.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.480
That might mean studying prairie dog burrows
to build better air ventilation systems, mimicking

00:02:59.490 --> 00:03:04.670
shark skin to create bacteria-resistant plastic
surfaces for hospitals, or arranging wind

00:03:04.670 --> 00:03:09.600
turbines in the same drag-reducing pattern
that schools of fish swim in.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:14.240
Designers get inspiration from a lot of different
places, but Benyus thinks many of them could

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.200
benefit from looking more at the natural world.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.280
So there's a lot of looking at what other people
have done.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:28.160
And what they do is, they look at all the others, and they get ideas.

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:34.720
They literally do, I mean, a lot of designers have lots of magazines that they look through,

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:38.440
they tear those out and they put them up on inspiration boards.

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.160
But they're looking at other human technologies.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.400
Her idea was simple: designers should get
in the habit of bringing a biologist to the

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.709
table, and let them help solve problems by
mimicking nature.

00:03:50.709 --> 00:03:53.599
And there are three main ways they can do
that.

00:03:53.599 --> 00:03:57.170
You can mimic its form, or its shape.

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:03.489
You might create a paint for a building that,
when it dries, it's got the same structure

00:04:03.489 --> 00:04:10.900
as self-cleaning leaves, lotus leaves are
notoriously great, they let rainwater clean

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:15.750
the leaf because because they have
these bumps and the rain water balls up on

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.729
the bumps, and then it pearls away the dirt.

00:04:18.729 --> 00:04:24.100
So that lotus effect is physical, and you
can create a physical structure on the outside

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:25.100
of any product.

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:27.740
Imagine that on the outside your car, rainwater
would clean your car.

00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:29.960
So that's mimicking form.

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:33.840
But there's also mimicking process, the processes of the natural world.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:39.640
It might even be how you mimic how ants communicate in order to efficiently

00:04:39.640 --> 00:04:48.640
find sources of food or new places to live.
And those processes, that self-organization,

00:04:48.650 --> 00:04:53.890
has been mimicked in software, in things like
autonomous cars and how they're gonna move

00:04:53.890 --> 00:04:57.480
in flocks through the city by talking to one
another.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:00.360
That's mimicking nature's process.

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:06.580
And then you jump up to the level of mimicking
whole ecosystems.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:10.690
There's a thing that's a buzzword right now,
that's really hot, called the circular

00:05:10.690 --> 00:05:18.310
economy, which is essentially industries saying
there should be no such thing as a byproduct

00:05:18.310 --> 00:05:22.490
in a manufacturing facility that goes to landfill.

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:28.120
It should be used by something else, and at
the end of a product's life, that product

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.920
should be upcycled into something else. It's being called the circular economy.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:35.180
Ecosystems do that really, really, really
well.

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:41.120
You've got a log on the forest floor, and
those materials move up into the body of the

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:43.580
fungus that eats it. Those materials move up

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.620
into a mouse.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:50.760
And that mouse material moves up into a hawk...

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:55.760
And if you think about that as what we'd like to do with local materials being upcycled constantly.

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:57.760
In our cities, for instance.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.520
Those ecosystem lessons are really big for us.

00:06:03.280 --> 00:06:09.160
And that’s the end goal for biomimetic design
— making products, systems, and cities functionally

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:12.349
indistinguishable from the natural world.

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:17.160
Life has been around on Earth for 3.8 billion
years — and what designers are starting

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:21.060
to realize is that’s a lot of research and
development time.

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:26.210
The people who design our world have a lot
to learn from the natural world.

00:06:26.210 --> 00:06:30.130
All they have to do is take a look.

00:06:30.130 --> 00:06:34.060
Thank you so much for watching, this is one
of a series of videos that we're doing in

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.530
collaboration with 99% Invisible.

00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.620
They are a podcast that does stories all about
design.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:45.080
We loved working with them, you should definitely check them out at 99pi.org or on any podcast app.

